    "lawCode": "Amd §§168-b, 168-l & 168-q, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S962\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to the registration of\nsex offenders\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo make information about all registered sex offenders available on\nthe Division of Criminal Justice Services home page; to provide for\nregistration of automatic e-mail notification when a sex offender\nmoves into a resident's zip code.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection one of the bill amends subdivision 6 of section 168-b of the\nCorrection Law to change the reference to the subdirectory to a\nreference to the directory of sex offenders.\n\nSection two of the bill amends paragraphs a, b and c of subdivision 6\nof section 168-1 of the Correction Law to describe what information\nabout a sex offender is available on the Division homepage depending\non whether the offender has been designated a level one, two or three\nrisk.\n\nSection three of the bill amends section 168-q of the Correction Law\nto change the title of the Sub directory to the Directory and to\nauthorize certain information about level one sex offenders to be made\navailable in the Directory on the Division homepage in addition to the\ncurrent information which is made available on level two and level\nthree sex offenders.\n\nSection four of the bill provides that it takes effect 180 days after\nit becomes law.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\n\nCurrently, the Sex Offender Registration Act does not require all sex\noffenders to be accessible on the DOS directory website.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn New York State, a sex offender remains on the Sex Offender Registry\nfor at least twenty years. Many offenders are required to register for\nlife. A directory of Level Two and Level Three Sex Offenders is\nmaintained on the Division of Criminal Justice Services homepage.\nWhile this directory provides valuable information concerning those\noffenders who have been determined to pose moderate and high risk, no\nsimilar information is provided about offenders found to pose a low\nrisk of re-offense. A person must call the 800 number operated by the\nDivision to learn of low risk offenders in the community.\n\nTo use the 800 number, the caller must have the sex offender's name\nand either the Offender's exact address, date of birth, social\nsecurity number or driver's license number. There is no fee for the\n800 number. The 800 number has proved to be a useful. Screening tool\nfor certain business and volunteer agencies, however, it is not as\nuseful for a person who wishes to know who may pose a threat to the\ncommunity but does not know the specific identifying information\nrequired for an 800 number search. Making information about all\nregistered sex offenders based upon risk level, available on the\nDivision home page will expand the utility of the Registry and enhance\nthe safety of New York's most vulnerable citizens.  Individuals and\norganizations who need access to Registry information will be able to\nobtain such information from any computer with access to the Internet\nat a convenient time and will no longer be limited to calling the 800\nnumber which is only in service weekdays between 9 a.m. and 5 p.m. In\naddition, by providing a mechanism whereby residents may register with\nDOS to receive automatic e-mail notification whenever an offender\nmoves into their zip code will give individuals the peace of mind that\nthey will be timely notified whenever there is an individual who may\npose a risk living nearby.  Furthermore, it takes away the burden of\nhaving to check the registry all the time to look for new offenders\nliving in the community.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nS.4776A/A.6019A of 2015-2016; Passed Senate\nS.2017/A.2649 of 2013-2014; Passed Senate\nS.2005a/A.4871A of 2011-2012; Passed Senate\nS.1488/A.3793 of 2009-2010; Referred to Crime Victims, Crime and\nCorrection\nS.3713/A.8438 of 2007-2008; Passed Senate\nS.4968/A.4402B of 2005-2006; Passed Senate\nS.4007A/A.8321A of 2003-2004; Passed Senate\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\n180 days after the bill becomes law.\n",
